<span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">130</span><span class="mspace">&nbsp;</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.1389em;">mW</span></span></span></span></span>
</div>

<h2>Reading the Plot</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>vin</strong>: the 15 V sine wave</li>
<li><strong>out</strong>: the clipped wave, between −0.73 V and +5.18 V. The positive plateau is flat: this is the regulation effect of the breakdown</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Run It</h2>
<div class="ch-card">
<p style="margin: 0;">Press <strong>Start Analysis</strong> in the analysis block (Time Domain, 0 to 60 ms, step 0.1 ms: three periods of the source).</p>
</div>

<h2>Things to Try</h2>
<div class="ch-card">
<ol>
<li>Set <em>Vz</em> to 3.3 V (also in the symbol): the positive clip level becomes about 3.4 V</li>
<li>Change R2 to 100 kΩ: the clip level is almost unchanged (5.19 V), only a little less current flows in the load</li>
<li>Change R1 to 100 Ω: the current in Z1 reaches 142 mA, the clip level rises slightly (5.25 V) and the diode dissipates much more power</li>
<li>Replace V1 by a DC source with a ripple: the same circuit works as a shunt voltage regulator</li>
</ol>
</div>

<div class="ch-card green-border">
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">A single Zener diode with a series resistor limits a 15 V peak signal to the range −0.73 V to +5.18 V. The reverse breakdown fixes the upper level at V<sub>z</sub> and the forward junction fixes the lower level near −0.7 V.</p>
</div>
</div>

